# CrossEngin pattern pack: security_review v1
#
# Security-focused review heuristics for the coding_helper skill.
# Loaded via `pattern_pack_load("data/patterns/security_review.cerec")`
# at daemon boot (or over the wire when a `pattern.install` verb ships).
# Every pattern auto-attributes to src:pattern:security_review:v1.0.0
# so meta_observer output can track the pack as a unit.
#
# Curated from OWASP Top 10 (2021) + CWE Top 25 (2023). Patterns are
# ADVICE, not truth-claims -- they never enter a KG and never go
# through the review queue. Confidence levels reflect the
# "recognition strength" of the trigger tokens, NOT the severity of
# the bug (severity is a separate axis the guidance describes).

CAPSULE security_review
VERSION 1.0.0
DESCRIPTION OWASP + CWE derived review patterns for coding_helper
LICENSE CC-BY-4.0

# --- Injection family ---------------------------------------------------

PATTERN 900
TRIGGER sql injection
GUIDANCE Prefer parameterized queries / prepared statements. Never interpolate user input into SQL. If dynamic identifiers (table names) are required, allowlist them explicitly rather than escaping.

PATTERN 850
TRIGGER command injection
GUIDANCE Do not pass user input to a shell. Use the process-spawn API with an argv array (never a single string with shell=True). Validate on an allowlist if the input names a subcommand.

PATTERN 800
TRIGGER shell escape
GUIDANCE Shell escaping is a workaround, not a defense -- prefer the argv-array spawn path. If you MUST escape, use the language's dedicated shell-quote function; do not roll your own.

PATTERN 850
TRIGGER path traversal
GUIDANCE Resolve the user-supplied path with realpath / canonicalize, then verify it starts with the intended base directory. Never build a path with plain string concat + open.

PATTERN 800
TRIGGER template injection
GUIDANCE Render templates with a sandboxed engine that separates data from control. Never eval() a user-supplied template.

# --- Cross-site & session ----------------------------------------------

PATTERN 900
TRIGGER xss cross-site
GUIDANCE Escape user-supplied HTML at the RENDER boundary (never trust upstream sanitization). Use the framework's default template autoescape; opt out only for specific values that are already safe.

PATTERN 800
TRIGGER csrf
GUIDANCE State-changing endpoints need a CSRF token bound to the session (double-submit cookie or synchronizer token). GET requests must be idempotent.

PATTERN 800
TRIGGER session fixation
GUIDANCE Rotate the session identifier on privilege change (login, role escalation). Never accept a session id supplied by the client via query string.

PATTERN 750
TRIGGER cookie insecure
GUIDANCE Set Secure + HttpOnly + SameSite on session cookies. SameSite=Lax by default; Strict for auth-sensitive flows.

# --- Authentication & authorization -----------------------------------

PATTERN 850
TRIGGER hardcoded secret
GUIDANCE Extract to environment variables or a secrets manager. Check git history for prior commits of the same value; if leaked, rotate the credential.

PATTERN 800
TRIGGER weak password
GUIDANCE Do not enforce complexity rules that push users to reuse; enforce minimum length (12+) and check against a breached-password list (e.g. HIBP). Use a slow KDF (argon2id / bcrypt / scrypt).

PATTERN 850
TRIGGER broken authentication
GUIDANCE Rate-limit login attempts per account AND per IP. Require re-auth for security-sensitive operations. Support 2FA (TOTP or WebAuthn); never SMS as the primary.

PATTERN 900
TRIGGER privilege escalation
GUIDANCE Enforce authorization at the SERVER on every request, not just in the UI. Never trust a client-side "isAdmin" claim. Log denials.

PATTERN 800
TRIGGER insecure direct object reference
GUIDANCE Do not use raw database IDs in URLs when they identify user-owned resources. Either use opaque tokens (UUIDs) or check owner-of-resource == current-user on every access.

# --- Deserialization & crypto -----------------------------------------

PATTERN 900
TRIGGER unsafe deserialization
GUIDANCE Never deserialize untrusted data with a format that permits code execution (Python pickle, PHP unserialize, Java ObjectInputStream). Use a data-only format (JSON) and validate against a schema.

PATTERN 800
TRIGGER weak crypto
GUIDANCE MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for security use -- switch to SHA-256 or SHA-3. Do not roll your own encryption; use the language's high-level API (libsodium / cryptography.io / etc.).

PATTERN 750
TRIGGER weak random
GUIDANCE Use a cryptographic RNG (os.urandom / secrets / getrandom) for security tokens, session IDs, and passwords. Never seed with time().

PATTERN 800
TRIGGER hardcoded iv
GUIDANCE Never reuse an IV / nonce with the same key -- catastrophic for CBC, CTR, GCM. Generate a fresh random IV per message; prepend it to the ciphertext.

# --- Network & TLS ----------------------------------------------------

PATTERN 850
TRIGGER tls disabled
GUIDANCE Do NOT disable certificate verification (verify=False, --insecure, InsecureSkipVerify). Fix the cert chain instead. In dev, use a local CA + trust it explicitly.

PATTERN 750
TRIGGER open redirect
GUIDANCE Allowlist redirect targets. Never redirect to a URL supplied by the client without checking it against a known-good set.

PATTERN 700
TRIGGER cors wildcard
GUIDANCE Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * with Credentials is refused by browsers. If you need credentialed CORS, echo an allowlisted origin exactly; never *.

# --- Data exposure ----------------------------------------------------

PATTERN 800
TRIGGER sensitive log
GUIDANCE Do not log passwords, tokens, PII, or full request bodies. Redact known-sensitive fields at the LOG boundary; assume logs will be shared / archived / breached.

PATTERN 750
TRIGGER stack trace exposed
GUIDANCE Never render a raw stack trace to end users. Show a generic error; log the detail server-side with a request-id the user can quote.

PATTERN 750
TRIGGER debug endpoint
GUIDANCE /debug, /admin, /pprof, /trace, /metrics are commonly left open in production. Gate them behind auth + IP allowlist; better: mount them on a separate loopback-only port.

# --- Timing / side-channels -----------------------------------------

PATTERN 700
TRIGGER timing attack
GUIDANCE Compare secrets (tokens, HMACs, passwords) with a CONSTANT-TIME function (hmac.compare_digest / subtle.ConstantTimeCompare / etc.), not ==. Same for early-exit lookups.
